{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"459732415f4f6ef659048113f78ec23773ea58de24823c131156dc842a7fca4f"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's claim is conditional ('more resilient if operators are independently incentivized to maintain divergent practices'), yet it omits the problem's central challenge: identifying evidence that distinguishes truly independent failure modes from failures merely appearing uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. The mechanism argues that decentralized diversity prevents configuration-error propagation, but 'no observed propagation' is precisely the 'merely uncorrelated until a shared cause' situation the problem flags. If decentralized operators independently converge on the same popular library, cloud best-practice, or upstream dependency (as SRC_002's own countercondition acknowledges), a shared latent bug would remain invisible until triggered. The claim supplies no evidence criterion — e.g., stress injection, dependency-graph audit, divergence measurement under controlled perturbation — that would separate genuine independence from this latent common-cause convergence. By resting on an emergent-diversity mechanism without an evidential test for independence, it fails the problem's explicit requirement to identify distinguishing evidence.","counterexample":["A decentralized network where all operators independently choose the same popular TLS library; configurations diverge, so no configuration error propagates, but a latent bug in the shared library triggers correlated failure across all services — diversity of operational practices does not evidence independence of failure causes."],"target":"95e00257084c50e1204d743f860f00c7a5cbd11538dadebac5f35fa730113f16"}]}